Fixes #314. **Stacked on #315 (itself on #310):** the first two commits here are theirs. Once they merge, this PR shows only its own commit. The three PRs all change the Pen's drag handling, so stacking them avoids conflicts between them.

**Problem:** while drawing, a click on the last anchor appended a second anchor at the same spot (`path.appendAnchor`). The new anchor kept the old outgoing handle, so the path got a zero-length curve that left the point and came back, drawn as a spike about 22 pt long. In Illustrator, the Pen over the last anchor shows a convert mark:
- a click retracts that anchor's outgoing handle, so the next segment leaves it as a corner;
- a drag pulls a new outgoing handle out, leaving the incoming one.

**Change**
- `crates/tools/src/pen.rs`, when the button goes down while drawing:
  - on the last anchor, the Pen begins "Convert Anchor Point" and previews `path.setHandle {which: "out", independent: true}`;
  - the handle goes at the anchor itself (retracted), then follows the drag (Shift constrains it to 45°);
  - the button-up commits;
  - a new `handle` state holds this, and `busy`, Enter/Esc and the rubber-band overlay take it into account.
- A one-anchor path no longer closes on itself. Its only anchor is the last one too, so two clicks at one spot used to make a closed one-point path; they now leave it open.
- New `Cursor::PenConvert` over the last anchor while drawing, drawn as the Pen with a "^" mark (`ui-egui/src/cursors.rs`) and mapped to the crosshair like the other Pen cursors (`canvas.rs`).
- The module doc says so.

**Tests**
- New `pen::tests::clicking_the_last_anchor_retracts_its_handle_and_dragging_pulls_a_new_one`:
  - the cursor is `PenConvert`;
  - a click previews the outgoing handle at the anchor and commits, with no anchor added;
  - a drag previews it at the pointer;
  - a click elsewhere still appends, and the first anchor still shows the close cursor.
- New `pen::tests::a_one_anchor_path_does_not_close_on_itself`.…

## What and why

**Why.** Before this PR, opening an Affinity file in VectorCraft gave only the small, flattened preview picture that Affinity stores inside the file. Every layer, path, text frame and image was lost, and that low-resolution picture could be placed into artwork. People moving from Affinity Designer or Publisher, or using Affinity 3, need their documents to come over as editable vector art: layers, artboards, curves with live corners, gradients, text and images. This mirrors storytold/photocraft#1126 on the PhotoCraft side.

@callmeBalloch wrote a reader from public documentation of the format and from public sample files. Because the files are untrusted, it is bounded and panic-free. Anything it can't map yet is named in the import warning rather than silently dropped, and the preview stays only as a labelled fallback. Export is deliberately not part of this: nobody involved could open the files in Affinity to verify them, and an unverified writer would risk users' work (tracked in #665).

**What.** Affinity documents (`.af` from Affinity 3; `.afdesign`, `.afpub` and, recognised by their content, `.afphoto` from Affinity 1 and 2) now open and place as editable art instead of as their embedded preview:

- layers and sublayers, groups, artboards and Publisher pages;
- curves with live corners, parametric and compound shapes;
- solid and gradient fills, strokes;
- clipping, and vector and pixel masks;
- text, placed images and pixel layers;
- opacity, visibility, locks and blend modes.

Each kind of content that doesn't come in is named in the import warning. A file whose native data can't be read opens as its embedded preview, with the reason, and that preview can't be placed. Save never writes over the Affinity file, and there is no Affinity export (#665). Remaining import gaps are in #666. Related: storytold/photocraft#1126; sibling: storytold/photocraft#1607.

`.afphoto` isn't in the file-open lists, so Finder doesn't offer VectorCraft for Affinity …

## What this adds

A fourth Image Trace mode, **Flat Logo** (`mode: "logo"`, preset `Flat Logo`), for flat-colour logos.

The existing tracer follows hard pixel edges, so an outline can only be as good as the pixel grid. On a small or soft logo that shows up as faceted circles, chamfered corners, crooked stems and slivers. Flat Logo reads each pixel as a blend of the logo's flat colours, interpolates that blend smoothly, and places every outline where two colours are equally present, to a fraction of a pixel. It then cleans the outlines the way a person would:

- corners the blur rounded are put back (a line is fitted to the edge on each side of a bend and the lines are intersected);
- near-straight runs and near-circular arcs become exact lines and arcs (Snap Curves To Lines);
- the rest is fitted with as few cubic Beziers as the tolerance allows;
- touching colours overlap by 0.06 px so no hairline gap opens between independently fitted outlines;
- shapes are painted biggest first. Transparent areas fully enclosed by artwork (letters knocked out of a badge, the counters of an "o") are painted white so the logo reads the same on any background, or become real holes with Ignore White;
- an outline whose fit strays more than 1.4 px from the raw contour is fitted plainly instead.

Colours are found automatically, or named with `logoColors: ["#rrggbb", ...]` (not `palette`, which is Color mode's colour source). It works through the existing `imageTrace.make` command, so the panel, CLI, MCP and control channel all get it. Create: Fills/Strokes do not apply to it. Gradients and photos are refused with advice to use Color mode, because it would turn a gradient into patches.

```sh
vectorcraft-cli run --in logo.png --cmd select.all \
  --cmd imageTrace.makeAndExpand --params '{"preset":"Flat Logo"}' --export logo.svg
```

